From: John Stockdale
Subject: [PATCH] GOES HRIT DCS: survive a bad PDT, follow NOAA redirect safely, read the DADDS table

Four problems, all showing up together since NOAA moved the DCS site to DADDS (late 2026).

1. Deadlock. loadDCPs() locked dcp_mtx by hand and called std::stoul / std::stoi while holding
   it. When either throws, the mutex stays locked and the next dcp_mtx.lock() blocks for ever:
   the LRIT data decoder thread stops, and with it every product (imagery, EMWIN, DCS).
   https://dcs1.noaa.gov/PDTS_COMPRESSED.txt now answers 301 with a 161-byte "Document Moved"
   page, which was saved as the PDT; its first line throws inside the locked region. Log:
       (I) Loading PDTs...
       (W) Error loading PDT line for DCP processing: stoul
       (I) Loading HADS data...        <- nothing after this
   Stack of the hung thread: pthread_mutex_lock <- loadDCPs() <- initDCS() <- process().
   Fix: std::lock_guard, and parse before locking.

2. The PDT has moved, behind a redirect. It is now at
       https://dadds-dcs-api.data.nesdis.noaa.gov/pdts/PDTS_COMPRESSED.txt
   which answers 302 to a short-lived signed S3 link. perform_http_request() does not follow
   redirects, so that address would be saved as an empty file. The DCS updater now uses its own
   small downloader (dcs_tables.h): redirects are followed, but only over https, at most five
   times, with size and stall limits; http and other schemes are refused outright.

3. Anything was saved as the table. A download is now accepted only if it looks like the table
   asked for (at least 1000 PDT or HADS records), is written to a temporary file and renamed
   into place. A web page, an error document or an empty body never replaces a good file, and
   a PDT file on disk that is not a table is ignored with one warning instead of one per line.
   The tables are reloaded when either of them was updated, not only when both were.

4. The DADDS table differs from the old one in two ways the parser did not expect:
   - It is UTF-8 and 351 lines hold accented letters, which pushed every later fixed-width
     column along by a byte or more. Lines are converted to one byte per character first.
   - Positions are ten-thousandths of a degree (" 409581" is 40.9581), not degrees, minutes
     and seconds. Read as DMS they land up to about 0.4 degrees off. The form is detected once
     per file (minutes or seconds above 59 can only be the decimal kind); DMS still works.

The new address is added in front of the default pdt_urls. Existing settings.json files keep
their own list and need the address adding by hand.

Tested on 3618db33 (v1.2.3), aarch64, Debian 12, libcurl 7.88.1:
- dcs_tables.h alone against the live servers: http refused; the old address followed to its
  web page and rejected; the new address followed through its redirect, 43,400 records; a
  known station at the position HADS gives; all 43,400 lines 239 columns after conversion.
- In an isolated run (own HOME) with the "Document Moved" file on disk and the old address
  listed first: file ignored at start; old address refused; PDT and HADS updated; both
  reloaded with no line errors while the decoder ran; clean exit.
---
